Hi Lena,

Thanks for the clear summary. Answers below.

1. Who signs: the Vollmacht has to be signed for the GmbH by both Gesch=C3=
=A4ftsf=C3=BChrer, Jens Hartmann and Helen Ashworth, acting together, becau=
se that is how the company's power of representation is entered in the Hand=
elsregister. One signature is not enough, even on a bank form, and the bank=
 will check the signatures against the Handelsregisterauszug it already has=
. Chris cannot sign: he is not a Gesch=C3=A4ftsf=C3=BChrer of the GmbH, and=
 his role as group CFO is with Kestrel Engineering Ltd, the shareholder, wh=
ich gives him no power to represent the GmbH. His part is to approve: under=
 our delegation of authority policy, a new bank account and any Kontovollma=
cht need the CFO's approval before the Gesch=C3=A4ftsf=C3=BChrer sign. Plea=
se ask him to confirm his approval by email.

2. Notarisation: no. A Kontovollmacht does not need a notary under German l=
aw. The bank will check the signatories against the register and their ID. =
A notary is only needed for things like filings with the Handelsregister or=
 property transactions.

Please send me the bank's form before Jens and Helen sign it. I want to che=
ck the scope: which accounts it covers, whether there is an amount limit, a=
nd that payments need you and your colleague together, not one of you alone=
, which is how payment release works in the GmbH today.

If the form is fine, Jens and Helen can sign it in time for the bank's dead=
line on 6 November. Helen is based in Sheffield, so I can get her signature=
 here and send the form on to Jens in Dortmund.

If you will regularly need to sign for the GmbH beyond the bank, we could l=
ook at Prokura instead. That is a different route, and it is entered in the=
 Handelsregister through a notary.

